   801	   * ring.  HCPU's read pointer (read_idx_mirror) may still hold the
   802	   * non-zero value from the previous session -- dirty in DCache or
   803	   * stored in SRAM.  If we do not synchronise them:
   804	   *
   805	   *   circular_buf_data_len() = wrap(write_idx - read_idx)
   806	   *
   807	   * returns a large garbage value, and LCPU's fresh boot events
   808	   * plus ring garbage all replay as "new" HCI responses.  These
   809	   * ghost events are consumed by the host as replies to real commands,
   810	   * so HCI_Reset (0x0c01) is never actually executed by the controller,
   811	   * and bt_le_adv_start later gets "Command Disallowed" (0x07).
   812	   *
   813	   * Fix: flush DCache to write back any dirty read_idx and to load
   814	   * LCPU's current write_idx from SRAM, then advance read_idx to
   815	   * write_idx (discard ALL pending LCPU boot data), then clean the
   816	   * DCache so LCPU sees the updated read pointer in SRAM.
   817	   */
